select 'bbbb0004-0000-4000-8000-000000000026', v.pos, v.title, v.kind, v.body::jsonb, v.notes, v.mins
from (values
  (1, 'Diagnostic: where are you now?', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"The part of a neuron specialized to receive incoming signals is the...","options":["axon","dendrite","myelin sheath","synaptic vesicle"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Dendrites are the branching input surface; the axon carries the outgoing signal away from the cell body. See ''The neuron and glia''."},{"prompt":"A typical resting membrane potential for a neuron is closest to...","options":["+30 millivolts","0 millivolts","-70 millivolts","-140 millivolts"],"answer":2,"explanation":"At rest the inside of the neuron sits around -70 mV relative to the outside, mostly set by potassium and the sodium-potassium pump."},{"prompt":"The rising phase of an action potential is driven mainly by...","options":["potassium ions leaving the cell","sodium ions rushing into the cell","chloride ions leaving the cell","no net movement of ions"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Voltage-gated sodium channels open and sodium floods in, depolarizing the membrane toward positive values."},{"prompt":"Chemical neurotransmitters cross which gap between two neurons?","options":["the axon hillock","a node of Ranvier","the synaptic cleft","the cell body"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The synaptic cleft is the narrow space between the sending terminal and the receiving membrane."},{"prompt":"The central nervous system consists of...","options":["the brain and the peripheral nerves","the brain and the spinal cord","the heart and the brain","the sensory organs only"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The central nervous system is the brain plus the spinal cord; everything else is the peripheral nervous system."},{"prompt":"Which structure is most associated with balance and the coordination of movement?","options":["the hippocampus","the cerebellum","the amygdala","the retina"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The cerebellum, at the back of the brain, fine-tunes timing and coordination of movement."},{"prompt":"Forming new long-term memories of facts and events depends heavily on the...","options":["cerebellum","hippocampus","medulla","cochlea"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The hippocampus is essential for turning experiences into new declarative long-term memories."},{"prompt":"The body''s roughly 24-hour internal clock is governed largely by the...","options":["amygdala","suprachiasmatic nucleus of the hypothalamus","spinal cord","basal ganglia"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The suprachiasmatic nucleus in the hypothalamus is the master circadian pacemaker, cued by light."}]}'::jsonb, null, 10),
  (2, 'The neuron and glia', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Name the working parts of a neuron, trace a signal through it, tell the main neuron types apart, and say what each kind of glial cell does. Warm-up: from the diagnostic, which part of a neuron receives incoming signals?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"A neuron is a cell built to receive, integrate, and pass on electrical signals. Dendrites are the branched input surface. They feed into the soma (cell body), which houses the nucleus and sums the incoming signals. If the total is strong enough at the axon hillock, the neuron fires a signal down its axon, a long fiber that ends in axon terminals. There the signal is handed to the next cell across a synapse. Neurons come in three broad roles: sensory neurons carry information inward from the body and world, motor neurons carry commands outward to muscles and glands, and interneurons connect neurons to each other and do most of the processing in between. Glia are the non-signaling support cells. Oligodendrocytes in the brain and spinal cord, and Schwann cells in the periphery, wrap axons in myelin, a fatty insulation that speeds conduction. Astrocytes support neurons, help regulate the chemical environment, and shape the blood-brain barrier. Microglia are the resident immune cells that clean up debris. The gaps in the myelin, the nodes of Ranvier, let the signal jump from node to node, which is far faster than crawling along a bare fiber."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Trace a reflex touch: a sensory neuron in your fingertip fires, its signal reaches interneurons in the spinal cord, and motor neurons then drive the muscle to pull back. Input arrives on dendrites, integration happens in the soma, output leaves along the axon. Why myelin matters: a myelinated axon can conduct many times faster than an unmyelinated one of the same width, because the signal regenerates only at the nodes rather than continuously. Counting roles: in the eye, cells that detect light are sensory neurons; a cell that tells a leg muscle to contract is a motor neuron; a cell buried in the cortex linking two other neurons is an interneuron."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Which neuron part carries the signal away from the cell body? 2) What cell type makes myelin in the brain and spinal cord? 3) A neuron that commands a muscle is which of the three types? 4) What are the gaps in the myelin sheath called, and what do they do? 5) Which glial cells act as the brain''s immune housekeepers? Answers: 1) the axon. 2) oligodendrocytes (Schwann cells do this in the peripheral nervous system). 3) a motor neuron. 4) nodes of Ranvier; the signal jumps between them, speeding conduction. 5) microglia."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: thinking dendrites send the output; they receive, the axon sends. Mistake: treating glia as mere filler; they insulate, feed, and defend, and loss of myelin can badly slow signaling. Exit: (a) In which direction does a signal normally travel, dendrite-to-axon or axon-to-dendrite within one neuron? (b) Name the fatty insulation on axons. Answers: dendrite to axon (input to output); myelin."}]}'::jsonb, 'Neurons carry the signals; glia support, insulate, feed, and defend them. Both matter, and the brain has enormous numbers of each.', 13),
  (3, 'The membrane potential and the action potential', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Explain why a resting neuron is negative inside, describe the phases of an action potential in order, and apply the all-or-none rule and the refractory period. Warm-up: roughly what is a neuron''s resting potential in millivolts?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"At rest the inside of a neuron is about -70 mV relative to the outside. Two things set this: potassium tends to leak out down its concentration gradient, and the sodium-potassium pump pushes three sodium ions out for every two potassium ions it pulls in, keeping sodium high outside and potassium high inside. An action potential is a brief, self-propagating flip of this voltage. When input depolarizes the membrane to about -55 mV, the threshold, voltage-gated sodium channels snap open. Sodium rushes in and the inside shoots toward positive, the rising phase. Those sodium channels then inactivate while voltage-gated potassium channels open, letting potassium out, which drives the voltage back down, the falling phase. Potassium leaving briefly overshoots into hyperpolarization before the resting state is restored. During the refractory period the recently fired patch cannot fire again immediately, which forces the signal to travel one way down the axon. Crucially the action potential is all-or-none: any stimulus past threshold produces the same full-size spike, and a stronger stimulus is signaled by firing more spikes per second, not bigger ones."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Threshold check: if threshold is -55 mV and a small input lifts the membrane from -70 mV to -60 mV, no spike fires; a larger input reaching -50 mV crosses threshold and fires a full action potential. Direction of flow: the refractory patch behind the spike cannot re-fire, so a spike that starts at the axon hillock marches toward the terminals and not backward. Coding intensity: a bright light or a hard press does not make each spike larger, it makes the neuron fire at a higher rate, say 80 spikes per second instead of 10. Ion bookkeeping: sodium in causes the rise, potassium out causes the fall, and the pump quietly restores the gradients afterward."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Which ion entering the cell produces the rising phase? 2) At about what voltage is threshold typically reached? 3) If a stimulus is doubled in strength, does each action potential get bigger, or do spikes come faster? 4) What does the refractory period ensure about the direction of travel? 5) Which pump maintains the resting gradients, and in what ratio? Answers: 1) sodium. 2) about -55 mV. 3) spikes come faster; each spike is the same size (all-or-none). 4) it keeps the signal moving one way, toward the terminals. 5) the sodium-potassium pump, three sodium out for every two potassium in."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: thinking a stronger stimulus makes a taller action potential; size is fixed, rate carries strength. Mistake: mixing up the phases; sodium in for the rise, potassium out for the fall. Exit: (a) True or false: a below-threshold stimulus still produces a small action potential. (b) What restores the ion gradients after firing? Answers: false, it produces no action potential at all; the sodium-potassium pump."}]}'::jsonb, 'An action potential is all-or-none: past threshold it fires at full size every time; below threshold it does not fire at all. Strength is coded by how often, not how big.', 14),
  (4, 'Synaptic transmission and neurotransmitters', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Describe how a signal crosses a synapse, tell excitatory from inhibitory effects, name major neurotransmitters, and explain how the signal is switched off. Warm-up: what is the name of the gap the neurotransmitter crosses?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Most neurons talk to each other chemically. When an action potential reaches an axon terminal, voltage-gated calcium channels open and calcium enters. That calcium triggers synaptic vesicles to fuse with the membrane and release neurotransmitter into the synaptic cleft. The neurotransmitter diffuses across and binds receptors on the postsynaptic membrane. An excitatory signal (an EPSP, excitatory postsynaptic potential) depolarizes the receiving neuron toward threshold; an inhibitory signal (an IPSP) hyperpolarizes it away from threshold. The receiving neuron adds these up: spatial summation combines inputs from many synapses at once, and temporal summation combines rapid inputs from one synapse over a brief window. If the sum crosses threshold at the axon hillock, the neuron fires. The signal is then cleared quickly, by reuptake back into the terminal, by enzymes that break the neurotransmitter down, or by diffusion away. Major players include glutamate (the main excitatory neurotransmitter), GABA (the main inhibitory one), acetylcholine (used at the junction with muscle and in attention), and the modulators dopamine, serotonin, and norepinephrine, which tune mood, arousal, reward, and movement. A drug that mimics or boosts a neurotransmitter is an agonist; one that blocks it is an antagonist."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Excitatory versus inhibitory: glutamate opening channels that let positive ions in produces an EPSP, pushing toward firing; GABA producing an IPSP pushes away from firing. Summation: one EPSP of +5 mV will not reach threshold from -70 mV, but ten such EPSPs arriving together sum toward -20 mV and easily cross a -55 mV threshold. Turning it off: acetylcholine at a muscle is broken down by the enzyme acetylcholinesterase, while serotonin is largely cleared by reuptake back into the sending neuron. Agonist versus antagonist: a caffeine-like blocker that sits on a receptor without activating it is acting as an antagonist."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Which ion entering the terminal triggers neurotransmitter release? 2) Does an IPSP move the neuron toward or away from firing? 3) Name the main excitatory and the main inhibitory neurotransmitter. 4) Two rapid inputs from the same synapse adding up is which kind of summation? 5) Is a molecule that blocks a receptor an agonist or an antagonist? Answers: 1) calcium. 2) away from firing (it hyperpolarizes). 3) glutamate is the main excitatory, GABA the main inhibitory. 4) temporal summation. 5) an antagonist."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: assuming every synapse is excitatory; inhibition is just as important for shaping activity. Mistake: forgetting the off switch; without reuptake or enzyme breakdown the signal would never stop. Exit: (a) What triggers vesicle release at the terminal? (b) Name one way a neurotransmitter is removed from the cleft. Answers: calcium entry; reuptake, enzymatic breakdown, or diffusion away."}]}'::jsonb, 'Excitatory input nudges the next neuron toward firing; inhibitory input nudges it away. Whether it fires depends on the running sum of both across space and time.', 14),
  (5, 'Checkpoint 1', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"Within a single neuron, a signal normally travels...","options":["from axon to dendrite","from dendrite to soma to axon","from myelin to nucleus","in both directions equally"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Input arrives on dendrites, is integrated in the soma, and output leaves along the axon."},{"prompt":"Myelin speeds conduction because the signal...","options":["moves continuously along the whole fiber","jumps between the nodes of Ranvier","travels through astrocytes","reverses at each synapse"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The signal regenerates only at the nodes, so it effectively jumps, which is much faster than crawling along a bare axon."},{"prompt":"The rising phase of the action potential is caused by...","options":["potassium leaving the cell","sodium entering the cell","calcium leaving the cell","the sodium-potassium pump stopping"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Voltage-gated sodium channels open and sodium enters, depolarizing the membrane."},{"prompt":"An action potential is ''all-or-none,'' which means...","options":["stronger stimuli make bigger spikes","every spike above threshold is full size, and strength is coded by rate","spikes shrink as they travel","only sensory neurons fire spikes"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Past threshold the spike is full size every time; a stronger stimulus raises the firing rate, not the spike height."},{"prompt":"At a chemical synapse, neurotransmitter release is triggered when which ion enters the terminal?","options":["potassium","chloride","calcium","magnesium"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Calcium entry through voltage-gated channels causes vesicles to fuse and release neurotransmitter."},{"prompt":"GABA is best described as the nervous system''s main...","options":["excitatory neurotransmitter","inhibitory neurotransmitter","myelin protein","reuptake pump"],"answer":1,"explanation":"GABA typically produces inhibitory postsynaptic potentials, pushing the receiving neuron away from firing."}]}'::jsonb, null, 9),
  (6, 'Organization of the nervous system: CNS, PNS, and brain regions', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Split the nervous system into its major parts, contrast the sympathetic and parasympathetic branches, and match key brain regions to their jobs. Warm-up: which two structures make up the central nervous system?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"The nervous system divides into the central nervous system (the brain and spinal cord) and the peripheral nervous system (all the nerves outside it). The peripheral system splits into the somatic nervous system, which carries sensation in and voluntary muscle commands out, and the autonomic nervous system, which runs involuntary organs. The autonomic system has two opposing branches: the sympathetic branch mobilizes the body for action, the classic fight-or-flight response that speeds the heart and widens the pupils, while the parasympathetic branch handles rest-and-digest, slowing the heart and supporting digestion. Inside the brain, the hindbrain includes the medulla (breathing and heartbeat), the pons, and the cerebellum (coordination and balance). The midbrain relays sensory and motor signals. The forebrain includes the thalamus, a relay station for most sensory input, the hypothalamus, which governs drives and hormones and homeostasis, the limbic structures such as the hippocampus (memory) and amygdala (emotion), and the cerebral cortex. The cortex has four lobes: the frontal lobe (planning, decision-making, and voluntary movement), the parietal lobe (touch and body position), the temporal lobe (hearing and language), and the occipital lobe (vision)."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Branch check: your heart pounding before a race is sympathetic activation; the calm, slowed heartbeat afterward is parasympathetic. Region matching: difficulty with balance points toward the cerebellum; a problem forming new memories points toward the hippocampus; loss of vision in an intact eye can point toward the occipital lobe. Relay logic: a sound you hear was first relayed through the thalamus before reaching the temporal lobe, since the thalamus routes most senses (smell is the main exception). Homeostasis: feeling hungry, thirsty, or too warm engages the hypothalamus, which nudges the body back toward balance."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) The spinal cord belongs to which major division? 2) Which autonomic branch prepares the body for fight-or-flight? 3) Which lobe of the cortex mainly processes vision? 4) Which structure relays most sensory information to the cortex? 5) Which small region governs hunger, thirst, temperature, and hormones? Answers: 1) the central nervous system. 2) the sympathetic branch. 3) the occipital lobe. 4) the thalamus. 5) the hypothalamus."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: calling nerves in the arm part of the central system; only the brain and spinal cord are central. Mistake: swapping the autonomic branches; sympathetic ramps up, parasympathetic calms down. Exit: (a) Name the two parts of the central nervous system. (b) Which lobe handles planning and voluntary movement? Answers: brain and spinal cord; the frontal lobe."}]}'::jsonb, 'Two divisions of the peripheral system: the somatic controls voluntary muscle; the autonomic runs automatic organs, split into sympathetic (arousal) and parasympathetic (calm).', 14),
  (7, 'Sensory systems and motor control', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Explain how vision and hearing turn physical energy into neural signals, trace each pathway to its cortex, and describe how the brain plans and coordinates movement. Warm-up: which cortical lobe did we say handles vision?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Every sense begins with transduction, converting a physical stimulus into neural signals. In vision, light passes through the lens and lands on the retina at the back of the eye, where photoreceptors respond: rods are highly sensitive and handle dim light and night vision, while cones need more light and provide color and sharp detail. Their signals leave through the optic nerve, pass through the thalamus, and reach the primary visual cortex in the occipital lobe, which begins pulling out edges, motion, and form. In hearing, sound waves vibrate the eardrum and tiny middle-ear bones, setting fluid moving in the coiled cochlea. Hair cells along the basilar membrane bend and fire; because the membrane is tuned along its length, high pitches excite one end and low pitches the other, a tonotopic map. Those signals travel the auditory nerve to the temporal lobe. On the output side, movement is planned and launched by the motor cortex in the frontal lobe (the precentral gyrus), whose commands descend the corticospinal tract to motor neurons that drive muscles. The cerebellum times and smooths movement, and the basal ganglia help select and initiate actions. A single motor neuron plus the muscle fibers it controls is a motor unit; the lower motor neuron is the final common path to the muscle."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Rods versus cones: reading small print in bright light relies on cones; seeing a dim shape on a dark night relies on rods, which is why colors wash out at night. Pathway trace: a flash of light travels retina, optic nerve, thalamus, occipital cortex; a spoken word travels cochlea, auditory nerve, temporal cortex. Tonotopic idea: a high whistle and a low hum stimulate different positions along the basilar membrane, so pitch is partly a matter of place. Motor trace: deciding to lift a cup engages the frontal motor cortex, the command runs down the corticospinal tract to a lower motor neuron, and the cerebellum keeps the reach smooth and on target."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Which photoreceptors specialize in dim light and night vision? 2) Which lobe receives auditory signals? 3) What is the general term for converting a physical stimulus into neural signals? 4) Which brain structure mainly smooths and times movement? 5) A motor neuron plus the muscle fibers it controls is called what? Answers: 1) rods. 2) the temporal lobe. 3) transduction. 4) the cerebellum. 5) a motor unit."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: thinking cones handle night vision; rods do, cones need brighter light and give color. Mistake: assuming the cerebellum starts movements; the motor cortex issues commands, the cerebellum coordinates them. Exit: (a) Which lobe holds the primary visual cortex? (b) Name the receptor cells in the cochlea that transduce sound. Answers: the occipital lobe; hair cells."}]}'::jsonb, 'Transduction is the shared trick of every sense: convert a physical stimulus (light, sound, pressure) into the electrical language of neurons.', 15),
  (8, 'The behaving brain: plasticity, memory, emotion, sleep, and how we study it', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Explain long-term potentiation and Hebbian learning, name the circuits behind emotion and motivation, describe the sleep cycle and the circadian clock, and list the main tools and a few disorders neuroscientists study. Warm-up: which structure is essential for forming new declarative memories?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Learning changes the brain through synaptic plasticity. Hebb''s rule is the slogan ''cells that fire together wire together'': when one neuron repeatedly helps fire another, their connection strengthens. Long-term potentiation (LTP) is the best-studied form. In many circuits it depends on the NMDA receptor, which only lets calcium in when the sending and receiving neurons are active at the same time; that calcium signal then makes the synapse lastingly stronger. The hippocampus uses such plasticity to build new declarative memories (facts and events), while procedural skills lean more on the cerebellum and basal ganglia. Emotion and motivation ride on their own circuits: the amygdala tags things as threatening and drives fear, while the reward pathway, dopamine neurons running from the ventral tegmental area to the nucleus accumbens, signals reward and reinforces behavior. The hypothalamus turns bodily needs into drives such as hunger and thirst, keeping the body in homeostasis. Sleep runs in cycles of NREM stages 1 to 3, deepening into slow-wave sleep, alternating with REM sleep, when the brain is active and most vivid dreaming occurs; sleep supports memory consolidation. The timing of sleep follows a circadian rhythm set by the suprachiasmatic nucleus, which reads light and cues the hormone melatonin at night. To study all this, neuroscientists record electrical activity with EEG, watch blood-flow changes with fMRI, test what a damaged region no longer does in lesion studies, and probe circuits with stimulation. That work also informs understanding of conditions such as Parkinson''s disease (loss of dopamine neurons affecting movement), Alzheimer''s disease (progressive memory loss), and stroke (damage from interrupted blood flow). This is educational background, not medical advice."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"LTP logic: if two neurons fire together often, the NMDA receptor admits calcium and the synapse strengthens, so the same input later produces a bigger response, the physical trace of a memory. Memory split: remembering that Paris is a capital is declarative (hippocampus-dependent); riding a bike smoothly is procedural (cerebellum and basal ganglia). Reward: a rewarding event boosts dopamine along the pathway to the nucleus accumbens, making the behavior more likely to repeat. Sleep and clock: bright evening light can delay the suprachiasmatic clock and push back melatonin, shifting sleep later. Method matching: to see which region activates during a task you would use fMRI; to time fast electrical rhythms like sleep stages you would use EEG."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) State Hebb''s rule in one short phrase. 2) Which receptor is central to many forms of LTP because it admits calcium only during joint activity? 3) Which structure tags threats and drives fear? 4) During which sleep phase is vivid dreaming most common? 5) Which method would you use to measure fast electrical brain rhythms? Answers: 1) ''cells that fire together wire together.'' 2) the NMDA receptor. 3) the amygdala. 4) REM sleep. 5) EEG (electroencephalography)."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: treating memory as a single system; declarative and procedural memory rely on different structures. Mistake: assuming fMRI measures electrical activity directly; it tracks blood-flow changes as a proxy, while EEG measures electrical signals. Exit: (a) What does the suprachiasmatic nucleus control? (b) Long-term potentiation is an example of what general property of synapses? Answers: the circadian (roughly 24-hour) clock; synaptic plasticity."}]}'::jsonb, 'Plasticity is the through-line: experience physically changes synapses, and that change is how learning, memory, and recovery happen.', 16),
  (9, 'Checkpoint 2', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"The sympathetic branch of the autonomic nervous system is responsible for...","options":["rest-and-digest calming","fight-or-flight arousal","making myelin","forming memories"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The sympathetic branch mobilizes the body for action; the parasympathetic branch calms it afterward."},{"prompt":"Most sensory information reaches the cortex only after passing through the...","options":["cerebellum","thalamus","amygdala","spinal cord"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The thalamus is the brain''s main sensory relay station (smell being the notable exception)."},{"prompt":"In dim light and at night, vision depends mainly on...","options":["cones","rods","hair cells","astrocytes"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Rods are highly light-sensitive and handle night vision; cones need brighter light and provide color."},{"prompt":"Vivid dreaming is most associated with which sleep phase?","options":["NREM stage 1","NREM stage 3 (slow-wave)","REM sleep","quiet wakefulness"],"answer":2,"explanation":"REM sleep features an active brain and the most vivid dreaming."},{"prompt":"Long-term potentiation is best described as...","options":["the loss of a synapse","a lasting strengthening of a synaptic connection","the myelination of an axon","the resting membrane potential"],"answer":1,"explanation":"LTP is a durable increase in synaptic strength, a leading cellular model of learning and memory."},{"prompt":"The reward pathway that reinforces behavior relies heavily on which neurotransmitter?","options":["GABA","dopamine","acetylcholine","melatonin"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Dopamine neurons projecting to the nucleus accumbens signal reward and reinforce behavior."}]}'::jsonb, null, 9),
  (10, 'Comprehensive quiz', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"Which sequence correctly follows a signal through one neuron?","options":["axon terminals, axon, soma, dendrites","dendrites, soma, axon, axon terminals","soma, dendrites, myelin, axon","synapse, dendrites, soma, nodes"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Input on dendrites, integration in the soma, output down the axon to the terminals."},{"prompt":"The sodium-potassium pump helps maintain the resting potential by moving...","options":["two sodium out for every three potassium in","three sodium out for every two potassium in","only potassium, in one direction","calcium into the cell"],"answer":1,"explanation":"It pumps three sodium out and two potassium in, sustaining the gradients behind the -70 mV rest."},{"prompt":"A stimulus below threshold will...","options":["produce a smaller action potential","produce a full action potential anyway","produce no action potential","reverse the resting potential permanently"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Action potentials are all-or-none; below threshold none fires, and above it every spike is full size."},{"prompt":"At an excitatory synapse, a postsynaptic potential that moves the neuron toward firing is called...","options":["an IPSP","an EPSP","a refractory period","hyperpolarization"],"answer":1,"explanation":"An EPSP (excitatory postsynaptic potential) depolarizes the neuron toward threshold."},{"prompt":"Which pairing of neurotransmitter and role is correct?","options":["GABA - main excitatory","glutamate - main inhibitory","glutamate - main excitatory","melatonin - main excitatory"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Glutamate is the main excitatory neurotransmitter; GABA is the main inhibitory one."},{"prompt":"The cerebellum is most responsible for...","options":["forming new declarative memories","coordinating and timing movement","processing vision","regulating hunger and thirst"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The cerebellum fine-tunes the timing and coordination of movement and balance."},{"prompt":"A spoken word reaches which lobe for auditory processing?","options":["occipital lobe","temporal lobe","parietal lobe","frontal lobe"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The temporal lobe processes hearing; signals arrive via the cochlea and auditory nerve."},{"prompt":"Many forms of long-term potentiation depend on the NMDA receptor because it...","options":["blocks all calcium entry","admits calcium only when sending and receiving neurons are active together","makes myelin","clears neurotransmitter from the cleft"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The NMDA receptor''s coincidence detection lets calcium in only during joint activity, strengthening the synapse."},{"prompt":"The suprachiasmatic nucleus of the hypothalamus is best described as the...","options":["primary visual relay","master circadian clock cued by light","main motor output pathway","site of vivid dreaming"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The suprachiasmatic nucleus sets the roughly 24-hour circadian rhythm using light cues and melatonin timing."},{"prompt":"Which method measures blood-flow changes to infer which brain regions are active during a task?","options":["EEG","fMRI","a lesion study","the corticospinal tract"],"answer":1,"explanation":"fMRI tracks blood-flow changes as a proxy for activity; EEG instead records fast electrical rhythms directly."}]}'::jsonb, null, 12)
) as v(pos, title, kind, body, notes, mins)
where not exists (
  select 1 from public.lessons l
  where l.course_id = 'bbbb0004-0000-4000-8000-000000000026' and l.position = v.pos
);
